```python
import numpy as np

def order_in(conc, rates):
    slope, intercept = np.polyfit(np.log(conc), np.log(rates), 1)
    return float(slope)

def classify(conc_nu, rates):
    return "SN1" if order_in(conc_nu, rates) < 0.5 else "SN2"
```

Taking logarithms turns a power law into a straight line, and a least-squares fit averages out the scatter: the test data give slopes of 1.005 and −0.001. In practice chemists also vary the substrate concentration (first order in both cases) and add the leaving-group anion (which slows SN1 but not SN2), because a zero order in nucleophile can have other causes, for example a nucleophile so concentrated that it is effectively the solvent.
